tel 1.0

base gastronomy
import soundness

info   hashing: digests of any value or stream, with the algorithm as a type and weak choices behind permits
detail
    _Gastronomy_ computes digests. The algorithm is a **type** — `Sha2[256]`, `Sha1`, `Md5`,
    `Blake3`, `Murmur3`, or corpuscular's checksums `Crc32`, `Crc64` and `Adler32` — so a
    `Digest in Sha2[256]` can never be mistaken for a `Digest in Md5`, and a typo in an
    algorithm's name is a compile error rather than a `NoSuchAlgorithmException`. Any value
    with a `Digestible` instance is hashed directly with `digest`: the primitives, `Text`,
    `Data`, the collections, and every case class and enum, whose instances are derived from
    their fields. A stream, a file or a directory is hashed as it flows with `checksum`,
    through `Checksummable`, in bounded memory.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import providers.javaBaseProvider
    import alphabets.hexLowerCase

    t"Hello world".digest[Sha2[256]].serialize[Hex]
    // t"64ec88ca00b268e5ba1a35678a1b5316d212f4f366b2477232534a8aeca37f3c"

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
    Person(t"Alice", 30).digest[Sha2[256]]
    ```

    Three decisions are always explicit, each an import by name (`import soundness.*` brings
    none of them in, since a wildcard import does not import givens):

    - **The provider.** A `Hashing` provider supplies the implementation of each algorithm, and
      is selected from the `providers` package: `providers.javaBaseProvider` is the JDK's
      `MessageDigest` and `java.util.zip` (and, off the JVM, the same pure-Scala code the
      Soundness provider uses, so the import reads identically on every platform);
      `providers.soundnessProvider` is the pure-Scala `SoundnessHashing`, the only source of
      `Blake3`, `Murmur3` and `Crc64`. Each algorithm's `Hash` given demands a provider with
      that algorithm's method, structurally, so `digest[Blake3]` under the JDK provider alone is
      a compile error naming the missing `Hash in Blake3`, not a runtime failure. Importing both
      providers is fine; the lexically imported `SoundnessHashing` then serves every algorithm.
    - **The permit.** MD5 and SHA-1 are broken, and the checksums and `Murmur3` are not
      cryptographic at all, so `digest[Md5]` needs `cryptoPermits.permitDisallowedCrypto` (or
      `permitDeprecatedCrypto` for SHA-1 alone) and `digest[Crc32]` needs
      `cryptoPermits.permitNonCryptographicHashes`. The permits are erased `Permit` evidence,
      resolved by `HashWeakness`; SHA-2 and BLAKE3 are `Concession.Acceptable` and need none.
    - **The alphabet.** A `Digest` is bytes, rendered with monotonous's `serialize[Hex]` or
      `serialize[Base64]` under an `alphabets` import; `show` renders it as Base64 under the
      `Alphabet[Base64]` in scope, and `inspect` is always lowercase hexadecimal with a `ᴰ`
      suffix.

    The `Digest in algorithm` type is often all that needs to say the algorithm: a field declared
    `Digest in Sha2[256]` fixes what `payload.digest` computes, with no type argument.

    Beyond digests, the library holds the shared **crypto permit vocabulary** — `Concession`,
    `Permit`, `ProcessingPermit` and the `cryptoPermits` package — which enigmatic (ciphers,
    HMAC, signatures) and telekinesis (TLS) also gate with, so one import such as
    `cryptoPermits.permitDisallowedCrypto` covers weak hashes and weak ciphers alike; the
    `Signing` interface enigmatic's signing ciphers implement; `Hash#hmacName`, the JCE name
    enigmatic's `hmac[Sha2[256]](key)` uses; `Feistel`, a Feistel network over 64-bit values;
    and, in its `multihash` component, the self-describing `Multihash` envelope of the
    multiformats specification, with `Multicodec` mapping each algorithm to its registered code.

    The components:

    - `gastronomy.core` — everything above but multihash. It compiles for the JVM, Scala.js and
      Scala Native: BLAKE3, MurmurHash3, MD5, SHA-1 and SHA-2 are pure Scala, and the checksums
      are corpuscular's accumulators. On the JVM `JavaBaseHashing` is backed by `MessageDigest`
      and `java.util.zip`; on the other platforms the same object forwards to
      `SoundnessHashing`, so provider selection is the same source everywhere.
    - `gastronomy.multihash` — `Multihash` and `Multicodec`, for content-addressing callers.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the `providers` and
    `cryptoPermits` choice packages merge with the umbrella's own, so a user writes
    `import soundness.*` and then imports a provider and any permits by name.

refer gastronomy.digest
refer gastronomy.Digest
refer gastronomy.Digestible
refer gastronomy.Hash
refer gastronomy.Sha2
refer gastronomy.providers
refer gastronomy.cryptoPermits
refer gastronomy.Multihash

entry .digest   the digest of any `Digestible` value, in the algorithm named as a type
  detail
      `value.digest[Sha2[256]]` feeds the value's bytes, as its `Digestible` instance defines
      them, into a fresh `Digestion` of the algorithm and returns the result as a `Digest in
      Sha2[256]`. It needs a `Hash in algorithm` — which each algorithm's companion derives from
      the `Hashing` provider in scope, so `import providers.javaBaseProvider` or
      `providers.soundnessProvider` is required — and an erased `Permit[HashWeakness[algorithm]]`,
      which is always available for SHA-2 and BLAKE3 and must be imported from `cryptoPermits`
      for MD5, SHA-1, the checksums and MurmurHash3.

      The type argument can be left to inference when the expected type names the algorithm,
      which is the usual shape in a data model:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import providers.javaBaseProvider

      case class Block(digest: Digest in Sha2[256], payload: Text)

      val payload = t"""{"amount": 100}"""
      Block(payload.digest, payload)
      ```

      The same bytes digested under successive algorithms, each with the import it needs:

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label SHA-256 needs only a provider
        code
            import providers.javaBaseProvider
            import alphabets.hexLowerCase

            t"Hello world".digest[Sha2[256]].serialize[Hex]
            // t"64ec88ca00b268e5ba1a35678a1b5316d212f4f366b2477232534a8aeca37f3c"
      frame
        label SHA-1 is deprecated, so it needs a permit
        code
            import providers.javaBaseProvider
            import alphabets.hexLowerCase
            import cryptoPermits.permitDeprecatedCrypto

            t"Hello world".digest[Sha1].serialize[Hex]
            // t"7b502c3a1f48c8609ae212cdfb639dee39673f5e"
      frame
        label MD5 is disallowed, so it needs the wider permit
        code
            import providers.javaBaseProvider
            import alphabets.hexLowerCase
            import cryptoPermits.permitDisallowedCrypto

            t"Hello world".digest[Md5].serialize[Hex]
            // t"3e25960a79dbc69b674cd4ec67a72c62"
      frame
        label BLAKE3 needs the Soundness provider, which the JDK lacks
        code
            import providers.soundnessProvider
            import alphabets.hexLowerCase

            t"Hello world".digest[Blake3].serialize[Hex]
      ##
      ```

      A value is digested whole; `checksum` digests what is *behind* a value (a stream's
      chunks, a file's content) without holding it, and `Digester` digests several values into
      one accumulator. Without a permit, the compile error reads _this operation uses a
      sub-optimal algorithm, key length or mode — or a checksum in place of a hash; import the
      matching permit …_; without a provider it names the missing `Hash in algorithm`.
  refer gastronomy.Digestible
  refer gastronomy.Digest
  refer gastronomy.Hash
  refer gastronomy.checksum
  refer gastronomy.cryptoPermits

entry .checksum   the digest of the content behind a value: a stream's bytes, a file, a directory
  detail
      `source.checksum[Sha2[256]]` digests whatever the source's `Checksummable` instance feeds
      — for any `Streamable by Data over Credit` source, every chunk of its stream in order,
      drained window by window so the digest is computed in bounded memory; for a galilei
      `Path`, a file's bytes or a directory's regular files — and returns a `Digest in
      Sha2[256]`. It needs the `Hash in algorithm` the provider in scope supplies and the
      erased `Permit[HashWeakness[algorithm]]`, exactly as `digest` does. The result is the
      same whatever sizes the chunks arrive in, including one byte at a time, since the
      `Digestion` carries partial blocks across window boundaries.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import providers.javaBaseProvider
      import alphabets.hexLowerCase

      val chunks = Chain(t"Hello ".in[Data], t"wor".in[Data], t"ld".in[Data])
      chunks.checksum[Sha2[256]].serialize[Hex]
      // t"64ec88ca00b268e5ba1a35678a1b5316d212f4f366b2477232534a8aeca37f3c", as for the whole text
      ```

      A single `Data` value's checksum equals its `digest`, since its stream is itself. A
      `Checksummable` which raises, such as galilei's path instance (`Io.Error`, and it takes
      the `DereferenceSymlinks` policy in scope), carries its `Tactic` in its capture set, so
      `path.checksum[Blake3]` must sit inside `safely`, `unsafely` or a handler.
  refer gastronomy.Checksummable
  refer gastronomy.digest

entry .Checksummable   how a value's underlying content is fed into a digest, for `checksum`
  detail
      A typeclass with one method, `#checksum`, which appends the bytes *behind* a value — not
      the value's own encoding, which is `Digestible`'s business — to an exclusive `Digestion`.
      The companion's `.streamable` instance covers every source of bytes, and galilei adds one
      for paths, so `checksum[algorithm]` works on a `Chain[Data]`, a stream, a file or a
      directory. A `Checksummable` may capture a `Tactic` (galilei's does), which is why the
      typeclass is not `Pure` and `checksum` takes it as a capturing parameter.
  refer gastronomy.checksum
  entry .streamable   any `Streamable by Data over Credit` source, checksummed by draining its stream
      Drains the source's stream region by region and appends each window to the `Digestion`
      with the windowed `append`, so a reusable buffer is hashed in place and never copied. The
      instance captures the `Streamable` it is built from.
  entry #checksum   appends the content behind a value to an exclusive `Digestion`

entry .HashWeakness   the `Concession` an algorithm carries: what permit `digest[algorithm]` needs
    A match type from an `Algorithm` to the concession a caller must hold a `Permit` for: `Md5`
    to `Concession.Md5`, `Sha1` to `Concession.Sha1`, and `Crc32`, `Crc64`, `Adler32` and
    `Murmur3` to `Concession.NonCryptographic`; every other algorithm — the SHA-2 family and
    BLAKE3 — to `Concession.Acceptable`, whose permit `Permit.acceptable` is always in scope.
    `digest`, `checksum` and enigmatic's `hmac` all require an erased
    `Permit[HashWeakness[algorithm]]`, which is how a weak hash fails to compile until a
    `cryptoPermits` given is imported by name.

entry .Digest   the bytes a hash produced, typed by its algorithm as `Digest in Sha2[256]`
  detail
      A `Digest` holds the raw `#data` of a hash, and its `#Form` type member is the
      `Algorithm` which produced it, written with prepositional's `in`: `Digest in Sha2[256]`.
      Two digests are equal when their bytes are, so a digest is usable as a `Map` key, and the
      algorithm is a phantom — it is not stored in the value, which is why a `Multihash`
      exists for a digest which must describe itself across a boundary.

      A digest renders through the base encodings, under an `alphabets` import, since it is
      `Encodable in Data`:

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Lowercase hexadecimal
        code
            import providers.javaBaseProvider
            locally:
              import alphabets.hexLowerCase
              t"Hello world".digest[Sha2[256]].serialize[Hex]
              // t"64ec88ca00b268e5ba1a35678a1b5316d212f4f366b2477232534a8aeca37f3c"
      frame
        label Uppercase hexadecimal
        code
            import providers.javaBaseProvider
            locally:
              import alphabets.hexUpperCase
              t"Hello world".digest[Sha2[256]].serialize[Hex]
              // t"64EC88CA00B268E5BA1A35678A1B5316D212F4F366B2477232534A8AECA37F3C"
      frame
        label Base64, which is also what `show` renders
        code
            import providers.javaBaseProvider
            locally:
              import alphabets.base64Standard
              t"Hello world".digest[Sha2[256]].serialize[Base64]
              // t"ZOyIygCyaOW6GjVnihtTFtIS9PNmskdyMlNKiuyjfzw="
      ##
      ```

      A digest reads back from hexadecimal text with `.as[Digest in Sha2[256]]`, under a `Hex`
      alphabet and a `Tactic[Serialization.Error]`; the text's length is not checked against
      the algorithm, as the algorithm is a phantom. `Digest(data)` makes one from bytes already
      in hand, and a digest is itself `Digestible`, so it can be folded into a larger value and
      hashed again.
  refer gastronomy.digest
  refer gastronomy.Multihash
  entry .apply   `Digest[Sha2[256]](data)`: a digest from bytes already computed
      Wraps `data` as a `Digest in algorithm` with no check that its length suits the algorithm,
      since the algorithm is a phantom type. `digest` and `checksum` build their results with
      it; a caller uses it for a digest read from storage or received from elsewhere.
  entry .showable   renders a digest as Base64 text, under the `Alphabet[Base64]` in scope
      `digest.show` is `digest.data.serialize[Base64]`, so it needs a Base64 alphabet imported
      by name — `alphabets.base64Standard` or `alphabets.base64Url` — and fails to compile
      without one. The debug rendering `.inspectable` needs nothing.
  entry .encodable   a digest as its bytes, `Encodable in Data`
      The instance behind `digest.serialize[Hex]`, `serialize[Base64]` and every other
      consumer of `Data`: it yields `#data`. It is also why a digest is `Digestible` through
      `Digestible.encodable`.
  entry .decodable   reads a digest from hexadecimal text, as `text.as[Digest in Sha2[256]]`
      Deserializes the text as `Hex` under the hexadecimal alphabet in scope, which decides
      which case is accepted, and wraps the bytes with `.apply`. It needs a `Deserializable in
      Hex` and a `Tactic[Serialization.Error]`, raised for text which is not hexadecimal; the
      length is not checked against the algorithm.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import providers.javaBaseProvider
      import alphabets.hexLowerCase

      unsafely:
        t"64ec88ca00b268e5ba1a35678a1b5316d212f4f366b2477232534a8aeca37f3c".as[Digest in Sha2[256]]
      ```
  entry .inspectable   a debug rendering: every byte as lowercase hexadecimal, with a `ᴰ` suffix
      Always available, with no alphabet in scope, so a digest can be inspected where `show`
      cannot: `64ec88ca…7f3cᴰ`. The suffix marks the digits as a digest, as the algorithm
      cannot be shown at runtime.
  entry #data   the digest's bytes
      The raw output of the hash, in the order the algorithm defines: 32 bytes for
      `Sha2[256]`, 20 for `Sha1`, 16 for `Md5` and `Murmur3`, 4 for `Crc32` and `Adler32`, 8
      for `Crc64`.
  entry #Form   the algorithm which produced the digest, an `Algorithm` subtype
      The type member prepositional's `in` names: `Digest in Sha2[256]` is `Digest { type Form
      = Sha2[256] }`. It is a phantom, carried by the type alone.

entry .Digestible   how a value's bytes are fed into a hash; derived for case classes and enums
  detail
      A `Digestible` value is one which can be appended to a `Digestion`: `#digest` writes the
      value's bytes into the accumulator, and `digest[algorithm]` on any such value builds the
      `Digest`. The companion supplies instances for the primitives (`Int` and `Long` big-endian,
      `Double` and `Float` by their raw bits, `Boolean` as one byte, `Char` and `Short` as two),
      `Text` (as UTF-8), `Data`, `Digest`, anything `Encodable in Data`, `Optional`, `List`,
      `Set`, `Sequence`, frozen arrays, `Map` and `Chain`, and derives one for every product and
      sum type, so a case class is hashed as its fields in order, with no serialization step:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import providers.javaBaseProvider

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      enum Shape:
        case Circle(radius: Double)
        case Square(side: Double)

      Person(t"Alice", 30).digest[Sha2[256]]
      Shape.Circle(1.0).digest[Sha2[256]]
      ```

      The derived encoding is a concatenation: fields contribute their bytes in declaration
      order with no separators or lengths, and a sum type contributes the index of its case as
      an `Int` before the case's fields. It is deterministic and portable across platforms, but
      not injective — `(t"ab", t"c")` and `(t"a", t"bc")` digest alike — so it is a fingerprint
      of a value, not a serialization. For an encoding with framing, digest the value's
      `serialize`d form instead.

      The typeclass is `Pure`: an instance may be handed any exclusive `Digestion^`, and holds
      no capability of its own. `#contramap` builds an instance for another type from a
      function into this one, as `Digestible.double` is built over `Digestible.long`.
  refer gastronomy.digest
  refer gastronomy.Digester
  entry .conjunction   derives a product's instance: each field in order, through its own instance
      Used by the derivation for any case class or tuple with a `ProductReflection`; every field
      type needs a `Digestible` of its own, resolved lazily so recursive types derive.
  entry .disjunction   derives a sum's instance: the case's index as an `Int`, then the case
      Used by the derivation for enums and sealed traits with a `SumReflection`; the ordinal
      distinguishes cases whose fields happen to encode identically.
  entry .optional   an `Optional[value]`: the value's bytes if set, nothing if `Unset`
      `Unset` contributes no bytes at all, so `Optional[Text]` of `Unset` and of `t""` digest
      alike. Not applicable where the type is already `Unset.type`-inclusive.
  entry .list   a `List[value]`: every element in order
  entry .set   a `Set[value]`: every element, in the set's iteration order
      A `Set` of the same elements built differently may iterate differently, so this is stable
      only for a given set value.
  entry .sequence   any `Sequence[value]`: every element in order
  entry .frozenArray   a frozen `Array[value]^{}`: every element in order
  entry .map   a `Map[key, value]`: each key followed by its value, in iteration order
  entry .stream   a `Chain[value]`: every element in order, forced as it is digested
  entry .int   an `Int`, as four big-endian bytes
  entry .long   a `Long`, as eight big-endian bytes
  entry .double   a `Double`, as the eight bytes of its IEEE 754 raw bit pattern
  entry .float   a `Float`, as the four bytes of its IEEE 754 raw bit pattern
  entry .boolean   a `Boolean`, as the single byte 1 or 0
  entry .byte   a `Byte`, as itself
  entry .short   a `Short`, as two big-endian bytes
  entry .char   a `Char`, as two big-endian bytes of its UTF-16 code unit
  entry .text   any `Text`, as its UTF-8 bytes
      Always UTF-8, regardless of any `codepages` import in scope, so a text's digest is the
      same everywhere.
  entry .bytes   `Data`, appended as it is
  entry .digest   a `Digest`, as its bytes, so a hash can be hashed again
  entry .encodable   any value `Encodable in Data`, as its encoding
      A catch-all over the `Encodable` typeclass: a type with an `Encodable in Data` instance —
      an `Hmac`, a key, a `Multihash`-shaped value of a user's own — is digestible without
      declaring anything more.
  entry #digest   writes a value's bytes into an exclusive `Digestion`
      `digest(digestion, value)` appends the value's bytes with `Digestion#append`; the
      accumulator is `Digestion^`, exclusive to the caller, and the instance itself is pure.
  entry #contramap   a `Digestible` for another type, through a pure function into this one
      `textDigestible.contramap[Person](_.name)` digests a person by its name alone. The
      function must be pure (`->`), so the instance built stays pure and usable in derivations.

entry .Digester   a sequence of digest steps over one accumulator, run when an algorithm is chosen
  detail
      A `Digester` is a pure function from a fresh `Digestion^` to `Unit`, built up by
      `#digest` one value at a time and run by `#apply[algorithm]`, which creates the
      accumulator for the algorithm's `Hash` in scope, applies every step and returns the
      `Digest`. It is how several values are digested into one hash without first concatenating
      their bytes, and the algorithm is chosen last, so one digester can yield several digests:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import providers.soundnessProvider
      import alphabets.hexLowerCase

      val digester = Digester(_ => ()).digest(t"Hello ").digest(t"world")
      digester.apply[Sha2[256]].serialize[Hex]
      // t"64ec88ca00b268e5ba1a35678a1b5316d212f4f366b2477232534a8aeca37f3c", as for the whole text
      digester.apply[Blake3]
      ```

      `digest[algorithm]` on a value is a one-step digester. The empty digester is
      `Digester(_ => ())`.
  refer gastronomy.digest
  entry .apply   `Digester(run)`: a digester from a pure function over the accumulator
  entry #run   the steps, as a pure function from a fresh `Digestion^` to `Unit`
  entry #apply   runs the steps in a fresh accumulator for `algorithm` and returns the `Digest`
      `digester.apply[Sha2[256]]` needs a `Hash in Sha2[256]` from
      the provider in scope. Unlike `digest` on a value, it takes no permit: the gate is at the
      value's `digest` and `checksum`, not here.
  entry #digest   a new digester which runs these steps and then digests another value
      `digester.digest(value)` appends a step for any `Digestible` value; the original digester
      is unchanged.

entry .Hash   an algorithm's implementation as a value, derived from the `Hashing` provider in scope
  detail
      A `Hash in algorithm` is what `digest`, `checksum` and `Digester#apply` resolve: it knows
      the algorithm's JCE-style `#name` (`SHA-256`), its HMAC `#hmacName` (`HmacSHA256`, which
      enigmatic's `hmac` uses), and `#initialize`, a fresh exclusive `Digestion` from the
      provider's `Hashing.Function`. Each algorithm's companion derives its instance from
      whichever `Hashing` is in scope *structurally*: `Sha2.hash` needs a `Hashing { def
      sha2(bits: Int): Hashing.Function }`, `Blake3.hash` a `Hashing { def blake3:
      Hashing.Function }`, so an algorithm the selected provider does not implement has no
      `Hash` and fails to compile at the call. The corpuscular checksums' instances live here,
      in `Hash`'s own companion (`.crc32`, `.crc64`, `.adler32`), since corpuscular cannot see
      `Hashing`; resolution is the same.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import providers.javaBaseProvider

      val sha256: Hash in Sha2[256] = summon[Hash in Sha2[256]]
      sha256.name       // t"SHA-256"
      sha256.hmacName   // t"HmacSHA256"
      ```

      `Hash(name, hmacName, function)` builds an instance for a provider of one's own.
  refer gastronomy.Hashing
  refer gastronomy.providers
  refer gastronomy.digest
  entry .crc32   `Hash in Crc32`, from a provider with `crc32`: both providers have it
      Named `CRC32`, with the HMAC name `HMAC-CRC32`. Digesting with it needs
      `cryptoPermits.permitNonCryptographicHashes`.
  entry .crc64   `Hash in Crc64`, from a provider with `crc64`: the Soundness provider only
      Named `CRC64` (CRC-64/ECMA). The JDK has no CRC-64, so `JavaBaseHashing` does not offer
      it and `digest[Crc64]` resolves only under `providers.soundnessProvider`. It needs
      `cryptoPermits.permitNonCryptographicHashes`.
  entry .adler32   `Hash in Adler32`, from a provider with `adler32`: both providers have it
      Named `ADLER32`. It needs `cryptoPermits.permitNonCryptographicHashes`.
  entry .apply   `Hash[algorithm](name, hmacName, function)`: an instance over a provider's function
      Builds a `Hash in algorithm` whose `initialize` calls `function.digestion()`. The
      companions use it; a user needs it only to add an algorithm or a provider.
  entry #Form   the algorithm this hash computes
  entry #name   the algorithm's JCE-style name: `SHA-256`, `SHA1`, `MD5`, `BLAKE3`, `CRC32`
  entry #hmacName   the algorithm's JCE HMAC name: `HmacSHA256`, `HmacSHA1`, `HmacMD5`
      What enigmatic's `hmac[algorithm](key)` passes to the crypto provider, so an HMAC over
      a gastronomy hash is available for every algorithm the crypto provider's MAC supports.
      For BLAKE3, MurmurHash3 and the checksums the name is `HMAC-…`, which no JCE provider
      implements.
  entry #initialize   a fresh, exclusive `Digestion` for one computation
      Each call starts a new accumulator: `append` bytes to it and `digest()` it once. It is
      `Digestion^`, so it cannot be shared or stored.

entry .Hashing   a hashing provider: a `Hashing.Function` for each algorithm it implements
  detail
      A `Hashing` provider supplies the implementation of each hash as a `Hashing.Function`, a
      factory for fresh `Digestion`s. The trait itself has no members: each algorithm's `Hash`
      given refines it structurally (`Hashing { def sha2(bits: Int): Hashing.Function }`), so
      providers are *disjoint* rather than ranked — `JavaBaseHashing` has `md5`, `sha1`,
      `sha2`, `crc32` and `adler32`; `SoundnessHashing` has all of those plus `blake3`,
      `murmur3` and `crc64` — and a missing algorithm is a compile error at the digest.

      A provider is selected by name from the `providers` package. `providers.soundnessProvider`
      is the `SoundnessHashing` object itself; `providers.javaBaseProvider` is the marker
      `Provider.JavaBase`, from which `.javaBaseHashing` derives `JavaBaseHashing` (and
      enigmatic derives its JDK crypto), so one import enables both. A provider of one's own is
      an object extending `Hashing` with the methods named above, bound as a given.
  refer gastronomy.providers
  refer gastronomy.JavaBaseHashing
  refer gastronomy.SoundnessHashing
  entry .Function   one algorithm's implementation: a factory for fresh `Digestion`s
    detail
        `function.digestion()` returns a new exclusive accumulator; a provider's method per
        algorithm (`sha2(bits)`, `blake3`, `crc32`) returns one of these, and `Hash` wraps it.
    entry #digestion   a fresh, exclusive `Digestion` for one computation
  entry .javaBaseHashing   `JavaBaseHashing`, whenever `providers.javaBaseProvider` is imported
      A given in `Hashing`'s implicit scope, derived from the `Provider.JavaBase.type` marker,
      so importing the JDK provider by name enables hashing without a second import. On the JVM
      it is `MessageDigest` and `java.util.zip`; elsewhere the same name forwards to the
      pure-Scala `SoundnessHashing`.

entry .Provider   the markers of multi-capability providers; `Provider.JavaBase` is the JDK's
  detail
      A provider which supplies more than one capability — the JDK supplies both hashing and
      cryptography — is selected through a marker: `providers.javaBaseProvider` is
      `Provider.JavaBase`, and `Hashing.javaBaseHashing` (here) and enigmatic's crypto given
      each derive their implementation from it. Single-capability providers such as
      `SoundnessHashing` have no marker; their `providers` given is the capability object
      itself.
  refer gastronomy.providers
  entry .JavaBase   the JDK provider: `MessageDigest` and `java.util.zip` hashing, JCE cryptography
      The marker `providers.javaBaseProvider` supplies. It has no members of its own.

entry .JavaBaseHashing   the JDK-backed hashing provider: MD5, SHA-1, SHA-2, CRC-32 and Adler-32
  detail
      The default provider, reached by `import providers.javaBaseProvider`. It is the one object
      declared by two components. On the JVM (`gastronomy.core` with its `core-jvm` sources) it
      is backed by `java.security.MessageDigest` for `.md5`, `.sha1` and `.sha2`, and by
      `java.util.zip.CRC32` and `Adler32` for `.crc32` and `.adler32`, all appending windows in
      place; it is the single home of `java.security` in the library. On Scala.js and Scala
      Native (`core-native`) the same five methods forward to `SoundnessHashing`'s pure-Scala
      implementations, validated byte for byte against the JDK's, so code selecting the JDK
      provider hashes identically on every platform with no source change.

      Neither platform offers `blake3`, `murmur3` or `crc64`: the JDK has no implementation of
      any, so a `Hash` for those algorithms resolves only under `providers.soundnessProvider`.
  refer gastronomy.providers
  refer gastronomy.SoundnessHashing
  entry .md5   MD5, as `MessageDigest("MD5")` on the JVM and the pure port elsewhere
  entry .sha1   SHA-1, as `MessageDigest("SHA1")` on the JVM and the pure port elsewhere
  entry .sha2   SHA-224, SHA-256, SHA-384 or SHA-512 by bit count, as `MessageDigest("SHA-<bits>")`
      `sha2(bits)` takes 224, 256, 384 or 512; `Sha2.hash` supplies the count from the type
      `Sha2[bits]`.
  entry .crc32   CRC-32, as `java.util.zip.CRC32` on the JVM and corpuscular's accumulator elsewhere
      The 32-bit value is written big-endian, four bytes.
  entry .adler32   Adler-32, as `java.util.zip.Adler32` on the JVM and corpuscular's elsewhere
      The 32-bit value is written big-endian, four bytes.

entry .SoundnessHashing   the pure-Scala provider: every algorithm, including BLAKE3, MurmurHash3 and CRC-64
  detail
      Selected by `import providers.soundnessProvider`. It implements MD5, SHA-1 and the SHA-2
      family in Scala (following RFC 1321 and FIPS 180-4, cross-checked against the JDK at every
      block-padding boundary), BLAKE3 (checked against the official vectors at every input
      length), MurmurHash3 (byte-for-byte Guava's `murmur3_128()`), and reaches corpuscular's
      CRC-32, CRC-64 and Adler-32 accumulators directly. It is the only provider with `.blake3`,
      `.murmur3` and `.crc64`, so a program digesting with those imports it, alone or alongside
      the JDK provider. When both are imported, this one, being lexically in scope, serves
      every algorithm; the JDK's native `MessageDigest` is faster for SHA-2 on the JVM, so a
      program which wants it there and BLAKE3 too should scope the imports.
  refer gastronomy.providers
  refer gastronomy.Blake3
  refer gastronomy.Murmur3
  entry .blake3   BLAKE3, 256-bit output, from `Blake3.digestion()`
  entry .murmur3   MurmurHash3 x64 128-bit, seed 0, from `Murmur3.digestion()`
  entry .md5   the pure-Scala MD5
  entry .sha1   the pure-Scala SHA-1
  entry .sha2   the pure-Scala SHA-224, SHA-256, SHA-384 or SHA-512, by bit count
      `sha2(bits)` takes 224, 256, 384 or 512; any other count is treated as 512.
  entry .crc32   CRC-32 (RFC 1952), corpuscular's accumulator
  entry .crc64   CRC-64/ECMA, corpuscular's accumulator; no other provider has it
  entry .adler32   Adler-32, corpuscular's accumulator

entry .Sha2   the SHA-2 family as one type: `Sha2[224]`, `Sha2[256]`, `Sha2[384]` or `Sha2[512]`
  detail
      `Sha2[bits]` is the algorithm type for SHA-2 at a bit count of 224, 256, 384 or 512, each
      a distinct type, so `Digest in Sha2[256]` and `Digest in Sha2[512]` do not mix. It needs
      no permit, being `Concession.Acceptable`, and both providers implement it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import providers.javaBaseProvider
      import alphabets.base64Standard

      t"Hello world".digest[Sha2[384]].serialize[Base64]
      // t"kgOwxEOf0eauWHiGYze3xTKs1tkmAVDIAxjoq4wnzjMBifjflPuJDfHSmP82Bifh"
      ```

      SHA-256 is the usual choice; SHA-512 is faster on 64-bit hardware for long inputs, and
      SHA-224 and SHA-384 are its truncations with distinct initial states. The multihash codes
      are `0x12` (256), `0x13` (512), `0x20` (384) and `0x1013` (224).
  refer gastronomy.digest
  refer gastronomy.Hash
  entry .hash   `Hash in Sha2[bits]`, from any provider with `sha2(bits: Int)`
      Derived in implicit scope from the `Hashing` in scope, with the bit count taken from the
      type; named `SHA-<bits>`, with the HMAC name `HmacSHA<bits>`.
  entry #Bits   the output length in bits: the type's own parameter

entry .Sha1   SHA-1, 160 bits: deprecated, and digested only under a permit
  detail
      SHA-1 is broken against collision attacks and remains for the formats which still need
      it, such as Git object names. `digest[Sha1]` needs
      `cryptoPermits.permitDeprecatedCrypto` (or any permit which contains it:
      `permitDisallowedCrypto`, `permitCryptoThrough2030`) in scope, since `HashWeakness[Sha1]`
      is `Concession.Sha1`. Both providers implement it; the multihash code is `0x11`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import providers.javaBaseProvider
      import cryptoPermits.permitDeprecatedCrypto
      import alphabets.base64Url

      t"Hello world".digest[Sha1].serialize[Base64]   // t"e1AsOh9IyGCa4hLN-2Od7jlnP14"
      ```
  refer gastronomy.cryptoPermits
  entry .hash   `Hash in Sha1`, from any provider with `sha1`
      Named `SHA1`, with the HMAC name `HmacSHA1`.
  entry #Bits   160

entry .Md5   MD5, 128 bits: disallowed, and digested only under a permit
  detail
      MD5 is thoroughly broken and remains for legacy checksums and compatibility. `digest[Md5]`
      needs `cryptoPermits.permitDisallowedCrypto` (or `permitCryptoThrough2014` or later) in
      scope, since `HashWeakness[Md5]` is `Concession.Md5`. Both providers implement it; the
      multihash code is `0xd5`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import providers.javaBaseProvider
      import cryptoPermits.permitDisallowedCrypto
      import alphabets.hexLowerCase

      t"Hello world".digest[Md5].serialize[Hex]   // t"3e25960a79dbc69b674cd4ec67a72c62"
      ```
  refer gastronomy.cryptoPermits
  entry .hash   `Hash in Md5`, from any provider with `md5`
      Named `MD5`, with the HMAC name `HmacMD5`.
  entry #Bits   128

entry .Blake3   BLAKE3, 256 bits: a pure-Scala hash with keyed and key-derivation modes
  detail
      BLAKE3 is a modern tree hash, faster than SHA-2 in software and extendable to any output
      length. The JDK has no implementation, so `digest[Blake3]` needs
      `providers.soundnessProvider`; it needs no permit. The algorithm's three modes are all
      here: the plain hash (through `digest`, or `.hashOf` for a chosen output length), the
      keyed hash `.keyedHash` for message authentication with a 32-byte key, and `.deriveKey`
      for deriving a key from a context string and key material. All three are checked against
      the official test vectors at every input length they cover.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import providers.soundnessProvider
      import alphabets.hexLowerCase

      t"Hello world".digest[Blake3].serialize[Hex]
      Blake3.hashOf(t"Hello world".in[Data], length = 64).serialize[Hex]   // 64 bytes of output
      ```

      The multihash code is `0x1e`.
  refer gastronomy.SoundnessHashing
  refer gastronomy.digest
  entry .hash   `Hash in Blake3`, from a provider with `blake3`: the Soundness provider
      Named `BLAKE3`, with the HMAC name `HMAC-BLAKE3`, which no JCE provider implements; a
      keyed BLAKE3 is `.keyedHash`, not an HMAC.
  entry .digestion   a fresh, exclusive BLAKE3 `Digestion`, 32-byte output
      What `SoundnessHashing.blake3` supplies: append bytes in any windows and `digest()` once.
  entry .hashOf   the BLAKE3 hash of a frozen byte array, at any output length
      `Blake3.hashOf(input, length)`: the plain hash, 32 bytes unless `length` says otherwise.
      BLAKE3's output is an extendable stream, so a longer length is a longer prefix of the
      same stream, not a different hash.
  entry .keyedHash   the keyed BLAKE3 hash of input under a 32-byte key, at any output length
      `Blake3.keyedHash(key, input, length)`: message authentication, where the key replaces
      the algorithm's initial state. The key must be exactly 32 bytes; any other length is a
      panic, _BLAKE3 key must be 32 bytes_.
  entry .deriveKey   derives key material from a context string and input, at any output length
      `Blake3.deriveKey(context, material, length)`: the context — a unique, application-specific
      text such as `t"myapp 2026-01-01 session key"` — is hashed in derive-key-context mode to
      a key under which the material is then hashed, so keys derived for different purposes from
      the same material are unrelated.
  entry #Bits   256

entry .Murmur3   MurmurHash3, x64 128-bit: a fast non-cryptographic hash, under a permit
  detail
      MurmurHash3 is the hash behind Guava's Bloom filters and hash-keyed structures: a few
      operations per 16-byte block, for inputs which are not adversarial, where a cryptographic
      digest's rounds would be several times the work for no benefit. It offers no collision
      resistance against an attacker, so `digest[Murmur3]` needs
      `cryptoPermits.permitNonCryptographicHashes` — the same permit as the checksums — and it
      is implemented by the Soundness provider only. The output is the two 64-bit words `h₁`
      then `h₂`, each little-endian, byte for byte what Guava's `murmur3_128()` produces.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import providers.soundnessProvider
      import cryptoPermits.permitNonCryptographicHashes
      import alphabets.hexLowerCase

      t"hello".digest[Murmur3].serialize[Hex]   // t"029bbd41b3a7d8cb191dae486a901e5b"
      ```

      A seeded digestion is `.digestion(seed)`; the provider's is seed 0. ulysses's
      `BloomFilter` digests through this type.
  refer gastronomy.cryptoPermits
  refer gastronomy.SoundnessHashing
  entry .hash   `Hash in Murmur3`, from a provider with `murmur3`: the Soundness provider
      Named `Murmur3-128`, with the HMAC name `HMAC-Murmur3-128`, which nothing implements.
  entry .digestion   a fresh, exclusive MurmurHash3 `Digestion`, with an optional seed
      `Murmur3.digestion(seed)` starts both state words at `seed` (0 by default), for a hash
      table which wants a per-instance salt; the result is 16 bytes. Nothing is allocated
      until `digest()`.
  entry #Bits   128

entry .Sha384   SHA-384 as its own algorithm type, with a multihash code but no `Hash` of its own
    A 384-bit `Algorithm` whose `Multicodec` instance shares the code of `Sha2[384]`, `0x20`,
    since they are the same function. Digesting is done through `Sha2[384]`: no `Hash in
    Sha384` is derived, so `digest[Sha384]` does not compile; the type exists for a value
    which names the algorithm without a bit-count parameter.

entry .Sha512   SHA-512 as its own algorithm type, with a multihash code but no `Hash` of its own
    A 512-bit `Algorithm` whose `Multicodec` instance shares the code of `Sha2[512]`, `0x13`.
    Digesting is done through `Sha2[512]`: no `Hash in Sha512` is derived, so `digest[Sha512]`
    does not compile.

entry .Feistel   a Feistel network over a 64-bit value, from a list of subkeys and a round function
    `Feistel(subkeys, round)(input)` runs one round per subkey: each round moves the low 32-bit
    word of the value to the high word and sets the low word to the old high word XOR
    `round(low, subkey)`. The round function may return any `Int`, negative included; the XOR
    is masked to 32 bits, not sign-extended. With no subkeys the input is returned unchanged;
    the rounds are reversible by running them backwards with the subkeys reversed, which is
    what makes a Feistel network a bijection on 64-bit values — a format-preserving
    permutation for obfuscating identifiers or sharding keys. It is not encryption unless the
    round function is a cryptographic one.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    def mix(value: Int, key: Int): Int = value*key + 0x9E3779B9

    Feistel(List(3, 5, 7, 11), mix)(0x0123456789abcdefL)   // 0xa7e731b19c3aeda5L
    Feistel(Nil, mix)(0x0123456789abcdefL)                 // unchanged
    ```

entry .Permit   erased evidence that a weak algorithm, key length or mode may be *applied*
  detail
      A `Permit[concession]` is the capability `digest`, `checksum`, enigmatic's `hmac`,
      `encrypt` and `sign`, and telekinesis's TLS configuration require before using something
      NIST SP 800-131A lists as deprecated or disallowed: `digest[Md5]` needs a
      `Permit[Concession.Md5]`, `digest[Crc32]` a `Permit[Concession.NonCryptographic]`. It is
      erased, so it costs nothing at runtime; it is a compile-time gate. A `Permit` is a
      `ProcessingPermit` too, so it also covers verifying and decrypting.

      `Permit.acceptable`, for `Concession.Acceptable`, is always in scope, which is why SHA-2
      and BLAKE3 need no import. Every other permit is imported by name from the
      `cryptoPermits` package, each an intersection of the fine-grained permits it grants:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import providers.javaBaseProvider
      import cryptoPermits.permitDisallowedCrypto

      t"Hello world".digest[Md5]
      ```

      Without one, the compile error reads _this operation uses a sub-optimal algorithm, key
      length or mode — or a checksum in place of a hash; import the matching permit
      (`cryptoPermits.permitNonCryptographicHashes` for CRC-32/CRC-64/Adler-32, or
      `cryptoPermits.permitUnauthenticatedCrypto`, `cryptoPermits.permitDeprecatedCrypto` or
      `cryptoPermits.permitDisallowedCrypto`) to allow it_.
  refer gastronomy.cryptoPermits
  refer gastronomy.Concession
  refer gastronomy.ProcessingPermit
  entry .acceptable   `Permit[Concession.Acceptable]`, always in scope: strong algorithms need no import

entry .ProcessingPermit   erased evidence that already-protected data may be *processed* with a weak algorithm
  detail
      NIST SP 800-131A distinguishes *applying* protection (encrypt, sign, hash) from
      *processing* data already protected (decrypt, verify), and allows the latter for longer:
      "legacy use". A `ProcessingPermit[concession]` grants only processing; `Permit` extends
      it and grants both. `cryptoPermits.permitLegacyCrypto` is the one purely processing
      permit, for Triple-DES and DSA, used by enigmatic's `decrypt` and `verify`; gastronomy's
      own operations all apply protection and need a full `Permit`.

      Without one, the compile error reads _this operation uses an algorithm whose status is
      "legacy use" or worse; import a permit (e.g. `cryptoPermits.permitLegacyCrypto` to
      process existing data, or `cryptoPermits.permitDisallowedCrypto`) to allow it_.
  refer gastronomy.Permit
  refer gastronomy.cryptoPermits
  entry .acceptable   `ProcessingPermit[Concession.Acceptable]`, always in scope

entry .Concession   the weaknesses a caller may concede: one marker per algorithm, mode, key size or TLS relaxation
  detail
      A concession names one specific thing a program accepts by importing a permit: a broken
      hash, a weak cipher, a short key, an unauthenticated mode, or a lenient TLS setting. The
      markers are sealed traits with no members, used only as the type argument of `Permit` and
      `ProcessingPermit`; all of them live here, in the lowest crypto library, so gastronomy's
      hashes, enigmatic's ciphers and telekinesis's TLS share one vocabulary and one set of
      `cryptoPermits` imports. `HashWeakness` maps each hash algorithm to its concession.

      The hashing concessions are `Md5`, `Sha1` and `NonCryptographic` (the checksums and
      MurmurHash3, which are not defective but offer no adversarial collision resistance), and
      `Acceptable` is the absence of any weakness. The cipher concessions, used by enigmatic,
      are `Des`, `TripleDes`, `Rc2`, `Blowfish`, `SmallRsa` (an RSA key under 2048 bits),
      `Dsa`, `Ecb` and `Unauthenticated` (any non-AEAD block-cipher mode). The TLS concessions,
      used by telekinesis, are `Tls10`, `Tls11`, `SmallDh` (ephemeral DH under 2048 bits),
      `CbcCipher`, `ExpiredCertificate`, `SelfSignedCertificate`, `UnverifiedHostname` and
      `UncheckedRevocation`.
  refer gastronomy.cryptoPermits
  refer gastronomy.Permit
  refer gastronomy.HashWeakness
  entry .Acceptable   no weakness at all: SHA-2, BLAKE3, AES-GCM; its permit is always in scope
  entry .Md5   digesting with MD5; granted by `permitDisallowedCrypto` and `permitCryptoThrough2014` onward
  entry .Sha1   digesting with SHA-1; granted by `permitDeprecatedCrypto`, `permitDisallowedCrypto`, `permitCryptoThrough2030`
  entry .NonCryptographic   digesting with CRC-32, CRC-64, Adler-32 or MurmurHash3; granted by `permitNonCryptographicHashes`
      Not a defect in the algorithm — a checksum does exactly what it claims — but a
      concession that no adversary is in the threat model, since none of these resists a
      deliberate collision.
  entry .Tls10   accepting TLS 1.0; granted by `permitLegacyTls`
  entry .Tls11   accepting TLS 1.1; granted by `permitLegacyTls`
  entry .SmallDh   accepting ephemeral Diffie-Hellman shorter than 2048 bits; granted by `permitLegacyTls`
  entry .CbcCipher   accepting CBC-mode TLS cipher suites; granted by `permitLegacyTls`
  entry .ExpiredCertificate   accepting an expired peer certificate; granted by `permitUntrustedCertificates`
  entry .SelfSignedCertificate   accepting a self-signed peer certificate; granted by `permitUntrustedCertificates`
  entry .UnverifiedHostname   accepting a certificate whose name does not match the host; granted by `permitUntrustedCertificates`
  entry .UncheckedRevocation   connecting without checking certificate revocation; granted by `permitUncheckedRevocation`
  entry .Des   DES; granted by `permitDisallowedCrypto` and `permitCryptoThrough2014` onward
  entry .TripleDes   Triple-DES; applied under `permitDeprecatedCrypto`, processed under `permitLegacyCrypto`
  entry .Rc2   RC2; granted by `permitDisallowedCrypto` and `permitCryptoThrough2014` onward
  entry .Blowfish   Blowfish; granted by `permitDisallowedCrypto` and `permitCryptoThrough2014` onward
  entry .SmallRsa   RSA with a key shorter than 2048 bits; granted by `permitDisallowedCrypto`, `permitCryptoThrough2014` onward
  entry .Dsa   DSA signatures; applied under `permitDisallowedCrypto`, processed under `permitLegacyCrypto`
  entry .Ecb   ECB mode; granted by `permitDisallowedCrypto` only
  entry .Unauthenticated   any unauthenticated (non-AEAD) cipher mode; granted by `permitUnauthenticatedCrypto`

entry .Signing   what a signing algorithm does: sign bytes with a private key, verify with a public one
  detail
      The interface enigmatic's asymmetric ciphers (`Rsa`, `Ecdsa`) and its HMAC adapter
      implement, declared here so that a library below enigmatic can take "something which
      signs" as a value. Keys and signatures are raw `Data`; enigmatic's `PrivateKey#sign` and
      `PublicKey#verify` are the typed, permit-gated front to it.
  entry #sign   the signature of `data` under `privateKey`, as bytes
  entry #verify   whether `signature` is `data`'s signature under `publicKey`

entry .providers   the hashing providers, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Which implementation computes a digest is decided by the `Hashing` given in scope, and the
      choice is never implicit: `import soundness.*` brings none of these in, and one or both
      is imported by name. The same package, merged in the umbrella, holds enigmatic's
      `opensslProvider`, and `javaBaseProvider` enables enigmatic's JDK cryptography as well as
      hashing here.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import providers.javaBaseProvider, providers.soundnessProvider
      ```

      Each algorithm's `Hash` is derived from a provider which has that algorithm's method, so
      the two are complementary rather than ranked: the JDK provider lacks `Blake3`, `Murmur3`
      and `Crc64`, and nothing is lost by importing both. With both in scope the Soundness
      provider, being lexically imported where the JDK one is derived in implicit scope,
      serves every algorithm; to use the JDK's native SHA-2 and BLAKE3 side by side, scope the
      imports with `locally`.
  entry .javaBaseProvider   the JDK: `MessageDigest` and `java.util.zip` on the JVM, the pure port elsewhere
      Supplies the marker `Provider.JavaBase`, from which `Hashing.javaBaseHashing` derives
      `JavaBaseHashing` (and enigmatic its `JavaBaseCrypto`). MD5, SHA-1, SHA-2, CRC-32 and
      Adler-32; not BLAKE3, MurmurHash3 or CRC-64.
  entry .soundnessProvider   the pure-Scala `SoundnessHashing`: every algorithm, on every platform
      Supplies `SoundnessHashing` directly. The only provider of `Blake3`, `Murmur3` and
      `Crc64`.

entry .cryptoPermits   the crypto permits, each a given to import by name: what weakness a program accepts
  detail
      Each given is an erased intersection of fine-grained `Permit`s (or `ProcessingPermit`s),
      named after NIST SP 800-131A's algorithm statuses — *deprecated*, *legacy use*,
      *disallowed* — or after a year, and covering hashes here and ciphers and TLS settings in
      enigmatic and telekinesis alike. The levels nest by inclusion: `permitDisallowedCrypto`
      contains every algorithm permit, and each `permitCryptoThrough<year>` contains the
      earlier years'. Import the narrowest one that admits what the program needs, so the
      concession stays visible:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import providers.javaBaseProvider
      import cryptoPermits.permitNonCryptographicHashes

      t"123456789".digest[Crc32]
      ```

      None is in scope by default; `import soundness.*` does not import givens, and a permit
      imported into a block with `locally` is scoped to that block.
  refer gastronomy.Permit
  refer gastronomy.Concession
  entry .permitNonCryptographicHashes   digesting with CRC-32, CRC-64, Adler-32 or MurmurHash3
      `Permit[Concession.NonCryptographic]`: states that no adversary is in the threat model —
      corruption detection, hash tables, Bloom filters — which is a different concession from a
      broken hash, so it is not contained in `permitDisallowedCrypto`.
  entry .permitDeprecatedCrypto   SHA-1 and Triple-DES: usable but transitional
      `Permit[Concession.TripleDes] & Permit[Concession.Sha1]`: NIST's "deprecated" status.
      The import for a program which must still compute SHA-1, as for Git object names.
  entry .permitDisallowedCrypto   every broken or non-approved algorithm, key length and mode, MD5 and SHA-1 included
      `Permit` for `Des`, `Rc2`, `Blowfish`, `TripleDes`, `Dsa`, `SmallRsa`, `Ecb`,
      `Unauthenticated`, `Md5` and `Sha1`: NIST's "disallowed" status, which subsumes every
      weaker algorithm permit. It does not include `NonCryptographic` or the TLS permits.
  entry .permitLegacyCrypto   processing data already protected with Triple-DES or DSA: decrypt and verify only
      `ProcessingPermit[Concession.TripleDes] & ProcessingPermit[Concession.Dsa]`: NIST's
      "legacy use". It is not a `Permit`, so it allows no new encryption or signing, and no
      gastronomy digest.
  entry .permitUnauthenticatedCrypto   any unauthenticated (non-AEAD) block-cipher mode, for enigmatic
      `Permit[Concession.Unauthenticated]`: every block-cipher mode but the AEAD ones, which
      enigmatic gates.
  entry .permitLegacyTls   TLS 1.0 and 1.1, small ephemeral DH and CBC suites, for telekinesis
      `Permit` for `Tls10`, `Tls11`, `SmallDh` and `CbcCipher`. Re-enabling these in the JDK is
      process-wide.
  entry .permitUntrustedCertificates   expired or self-signed peer certificates and hostname mismatches, for telekinesis
      `Permit` for `ExpiredCertificate`, `SelfSignedCertificate` and `UnverifiedHostname`.
  entry .permitUncheckedRevocation   connecting without checking certificate revocation, for telekinesis
      `Permit[Concession.UncheckedRevocation]`.
  entry .permitCryptoThrough2014   every algorithm NIST disallowed by 2014: DES, MD5, RC2, Blowfish, small RSA
      `Permit` for `Des`, `Md5`, `Rc2`, `Blowfish` and `SmallRsa`: the year-based alternative
      to the named levels. It admits MD5 but not SHA-1 or Triple-DES.
  entry .permitCryptoThrough2024   as 2014, plus Triple-DES and DSA
      Adds `Permit[Concession.TripleDes]` and `Permit[Concession.Dsa]`, disallowed after 2023
      and removed in FIPS 186-5.
  entry .permitCryptoThrough2030   as 2024, plus SHA-1: NIST's planned phase-out
      Adds `Permit[Concession.Sha1]`, so it admits every hash algorithm but the
      non-cryptographic ones.

entry .Multicodec   the multiformats code of a hash algorithm, for enveloping its digest as a `Multihash`
  detail
      A typeclass which *is* the mapping from an `Algorithm` to its registered code and name in
      the multiformats table: `Sha1` is `0x11`, `Sha2[256]` is `0x12`, `Sha2[512]` is `0x13`,
      `Sha2[384]` is `0x20`, `Sha2[224]` is `0x1013`, `Blake3` is `0x1e` and `Md5` is `0xd5`;
      `Sha384` and `Sha512` share the codes of their `Sha2` forms. It is deliberately partial:
      an algorithm with no instance cannot be enveloped, and `Multihash(digest)` does not
      compile for it. The checksums have none — Adler-32 is unregistered, and CRC-32 and CRC-64
      are registered as `hash`, not `multihash`, since the specification reserves multihash
      for cryptographic functions suited to content addressing; a caller who wants a checksum
      enveloped anyway can build the `Multihash(code, bytes)` directly from its code.

      Lives in `gastronomy.multihash`. `.name(code)` gives the registered name of a code this
      library knows, for describing a decoded envelope whose algorithm is not recoverable as a
      type.
  refer gastronomy.Multihash
  entry .sha1   `0x11`, `sha1`
  entry .sha2_256   `0x12`, `sha2-256`
  entry .sha2_512   `0x13`, `sha2-512`
  entry .sha2_384   `0x20`, `sha2-384`
  entry .sha2_224   `0x1013`, `sha2-224`: a two-byte varint in the envelope
  entry .sha384   `0x20`, `sha2-384`, for the `Sha384` type
  entry .sha512   `0x13`, `sha2-512`, for the `Sha512` type
  entry .blake3   `0x1e`, `blake3`: a draft entry in the table, but unambiguous and widely implemented
  entry .md5   `0xd5`, `md5`: a draft entry in the table
  entry .name   the registered name of a code this library knows, or `Unset`
      `Multicodec.name(0x12)` is `t"sha2-256"`; `0x00` is `identity`. A code outside the set
      above is legal and representable in a `Multihash` but has no name here.
  entry .apply   `Multicodec[algorithm](code, name)`: an instance for an algorithm of one's own
  entry #code   the algorithm's multicodec code, as registered in the multiformats table
  entry #name   the algorithm's registered multicodec name, such as `sha2-256`

entry .Multihash   a self-describing digest envelope: varint code, varint length, then the digest's bytes
  detail
      A `Multihash` is the multiformats envelope `<varint code><varint length><digest bytes>`,
      in which a digest crosses a boundary without the parties agreeing on its algorithm out of
      band — what content-addressed stores, IPFS, IPLD, libp2p and `did:key` exchange. One is
      built from a typed digest with `.apply`, whose `Multicodec` supplies the code, so an
      envelope can never claim an algorithm other than the one which computed its digest; and
      read back from bytes with `.parse`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import providers.javaBaseProvider
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val envelope: Multihash = Multihash(t"Hello world".digest[Sha2[256]])
      val bytes: Data = envelope.serialize   // 0x12, 0x20, then the thirty-two bytes of the digest
      Multihash.parse(bytes) == envelope
      Multihash.parse(bytes).algorithm        // t"sha2-256"
      ```

      The `#code` is a plain `Int`, not a gastronomy `Algorithm`: a received envelope may name
      a function this library cannot compute, and such a value is representable — comparable,
      forwardable — rather than an error; `#algorithm` names it when the code is known. A
      digest's length is explicit, so a truncated digest is a legal envelope. Two multihashes
      are equal when their codes and bytes are. The varints are the specification's unsigned
      LEB128, capped at nine groups. Lives in `gastronomy.multihash`.
  refer gastronomy.Multicodec
  refer gastronomy.Digest
  entry .apply   `Multihash(digest)`: envelopes a typed digest under its algorithm's code; `Multihash(code, bytes)` is the raw form
      `Multihash(digest)` needs the digest's algorithm to be a `Multicodec`, resolved from its
      `Digest in algorithm` type, so an algorithm without a registered code — a checksum — does
      not compile. The case-class constructor `Multihash(code, digest)` takes any code and any
      bytes, for an algorithm this library does not know or a truncated digest.
  entry .parse   reads an envelope from bytes, raising `Multihash.Error` for one that is malformed
      Decodes the code and length varints and takes exactly `length` bytes after them. The
      declared length must match the bytes that follow: fewer is `Reason.Truncated`, more is
      `Reason.Trailing`, since either would compare equal to nothing while looking
      well-formed; a varint running past nine groups is `Reason.Oversize`. It needs a
      `Tactic[Multihash.Error]`.
  entry .Reason   why an envelope could not be read: `Truncated`, `Trailing` or `Oversize`
      `Truncated`: _the data end before the declared digest length_ (or before a varint is
      complete); `Trailing`: _bytes remain after the declared digest length_; `Oversize`: _the
      varint exceeds the nine groups the specification permits_. The enum is `Communicable`
      with those messages, which `Multihash.Error` embeds.
  entry .Error   raised by `Multihash.parse` for an envelope which cannot be read
    detail
        Error 41, with the `Reason`'s ordinal as its detail number: _the multihash could not be
        read because `reason`_, where the reason is one of `Multihash.Reason`'s messages.
    entry #reason   which of `Multihash.Reason` applies
  entry #code   the algorithm's multicodec code, as an `Int`
      Any code the varint can hold, known to this library or not; `#algorithm` names the known
      ones.
  entry #digest   the digest's bytes, of whatever length the envelope declared
  entry #algorithm   the registered name of the code, such as `sha2-256`, or `Unset` if unknown
      `Multicodec.name(code)`: a decoded envelope naming a function this library does not know
      keeps its code and bytes and simply has no name.
  entry #serialize   the envelope as bytes: varint code, varint length, digest
      What is stored or sent; `Multihash.parse` reads it back, and the two round-trip.
